+
+# Decision: Concurrent Visit Edits
+
+## Context
+
+Twenty-four seasonal workers share twelve tablets across 320 plots, and a tablet can be offline for 48 hours. Workers compare field notes in the evening and correct the same visit independently, each starting from the state their tablet last fetched. Two submissions therefore arrive derived from the same starting state, days apart, with nothing in either submission indicating which worker is better placed to judge the plot.
+
+The record store has to say what the visit *is* after both arrive. That answer determines three things beyond this node: the metadata a tablet must retain and send, the shape of the work a coordinator does before Friday, and which visits are eligible for the partner's CSV. It is also the point where observations can be corrupted rather than merely delayed — a record that combines two workers' observations into something neither submitted, with no indication it happened, is worse for the science partner than a record that arrives a week late.
+
+The visit's scientific fields are observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, and notes. Relationships among them are real but not inferable from field names: an observer-name correction and a missing-species addition are unrelated, while a species identification and that species' cover estimate are tightly coupled.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Gate — no submitted edit is silently discarded** (inherited; HLD tenet *Do not silently discard a submitted edit*). An approach that resolves concurrency by letting one submission replace another without a record of what it replaced is eliminated, not scored down.
+
+Weighted criteria:
+
+- **Integrity of scientifically related edits** — *major*. Turns on the project's non-goal *automated botanical validation or inference of scientific intent*: the system is not entitled to decide that two edits are compatible. The question for each option is what happens when concurrent edits touch related observations.
+- **Inspectability from submission to exported value** — *major*. Turns on the tenet *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*. A coordinator answering the science partner's question about one number must be able to say where it came from.
+- **Coordinator review workload** — *moderate*. About 6,000 visits a season, two coordinators, who also answer questions about submitted observations. Review work that scales with the rate of concurrent editing rather than the rate of genuine conflict consumes the same two people.
+- **Timeliness of the weekly export** — *moderate*. Visits awaiting review are held out of the CSV, so review backlog is export delay, not merely coordinator inconvenience. This is a distinct consequence of workload: a backlog that never clears means data that never ships.
+- **Ongoing maintenance of the mechanism** — *moderate*. Turns on the tenet *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*. Rules that need revisiting as the field schema grows need someone to revisit them.
+- **Downstream contract complexity** — *moderate*. Tablet-sync and review-export are sized around this contract; what each option costs them is part of the option's cost.
+- **Tablet storage and submission size** — *minor*. Tablets hold drafts across multi-day disconnection on shared hardware.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+Each visit is a chain of complete revisions. A submission names the revision it was edited from. If that revision is the visit's head, the submission extends the chain. If it is not — because another worker's edit landed first — the submission becomes a second head, and the visit is contested: two complete, competing versions of the same visit, with no current record until a coordinator resolves them. Resolution is by selecting one head whole or by composing a new complete revision from both. The server never compares fields and holds no rules about them.
+
+**Demonstration.** Both workers fetch revision 3. One corrects a misspelled observer name; the other adds a missed *Salicornia virginica* record. Revisions 4 and 5 are both heads, and the visit is contested even though the two edits touch nothing in common. A coordinator opens the comparison, sees the two edits are independent, and composes revision 6 carrying both.
+
+**Analysis**
+
+- No discarded edits (gate): **passes**. Every head is stored as submitted and remains in history after resolution.
+- Integrity of related edits: **strong**. No combination happens without a coordinator reading both versions; the server has no mechanism that could combine observations at all.
+- Inspectability: **strong**. Every exported value traces to one complete revision, which names its bases, its author, and — when it is a resolution — which coordinator produced it and from which heads.
+- Review workload: **weak**. Review volume tracks concurrent editing, not conflict. The observer-name-and-new-species case above requires a coordinator even though no interpretation is in question.
+- Export timeliness: **partial**. Contested visits miss Friday until resolved, and the queue includes visits whose edits never actually conflicted.
+- Maintenance: **strong**. The mechanism is base-pointer comparison and head counting; adding a scientific field changes nothing about it.
+- Downstream complexity: **partial**. Tablet-sync retains a revision ID per draft. Review-export needs a side-by-side revision comparison with both resolution forms.
+- Tablet storage and submission size: **partial**. A whole visit per submission; tablets keep a revision ID rather than a base snapshot.
+
+**Summary.** Trades coordinator time for a server that cannot combine observations even by accident.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+Each submission carries the base state it was edited from and the changes it makes. The server compares each changed field against the base and the current record. Changes to disjoint fields combine automatically and the visit stays exportable; changes touching the same field are flagged for review. Merge rules are per field class: scalars take the single changed value, observer lists union, species entries merge by taxon key. The observer-name-and-new-species case resolves with no human involvement, and coordinators see only genuine overlaps.
+
+**Demonstration.** One worker revises a species identification from *Salicornia virginica* to *Sarcocornia pacifica*; the other, working from the old identification, revises that entry's cover estimate from 15% to 25%. Identification and cover are distinct fields, so a field-level rule combines them into a record asserting 25% cover of *Sarcocornia pacifica* — a figure neither worker estimated, with nothing marking it as combined. Making the species entry a single merge unit catches this pair, and moves the same question to the next pair of fields whose relationship the rules do not encode.
+
+**Analysis**
+
+- No discarded edits (gate): **passes**. Original submissions and their changes are retained alongside the merged record.
+- Integrity of related edits: **weak**. Correctness depends on the merge rules' field grouping matching the scientific relationships among fields. Where it does not, the system produces a combined observation and reports success. Requiring manual approval of every merged record closes this, at which point the option's workload advantage is also closed.
+- Inspectability: **partial**. Attainable, but it is field-level provenance — each value in an exported row traces to a different submission — rather than one revision a coordinator can read.
+- Review workload: **strong**. Only genuine field overlaps reach a coordinator.
+- Export timeliness: **strong**. Visits with unrelated concurrent edits stay exportable.
+- Maintenance: **weak**. Rules are a standing artifact: every new field class needs a rule, and every rule encodes an assumption about field independence that someone has to re-examine as the survey protocol changes.
+- Downstream complexity: **partial**. Tablet-sync retains a full base snapshot per draft and sends structured changes. Review-export needs field-level provenance and an overlap review view.
+- Tablet storage and submission size: **partial**. Base snapshot plus changes per draft; submissions are smaller.
+
+**Summary.** Trades a maintained body of field-independence assumptions for review work proportional to real conflict.
+
+### Last accepted submission wins
+
+The most recently received submission becomes the visit's record. No base state, no review step, no resolution workflow.
+
+**Analysis**
+
+- No discarded edits (gate): **eliminated**. The earlier submission's observations are gone from the record with no indication they existed — the failure mode the project is built to end.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Whole-visit revisions.**
+
+Both surviving options pass the gate and both are buildable. They separate on the two major criteria. Field-aware merge's weakness on integrity of related edits is not a defect in a particular rule set but a property of the approach: a rule that combines fields without understanding their scientific relationship will sometimes combine a species identification with a cover estimate measured for a different species, and will report that as a clean merge. That is the project's non-goal *automated botanical validation or inference of scientific intent* reappearing as a mechanism rather than a feature. Whole-visit revisions have no component capable of this, which is also why they are strong on inspectability: every exported value is one complete revision a coordinator can read, rather than a row assembled from several.
+
+The cost is real and lands on the moderate criteria. Review workload is weak and export timeliness partial: coordinators will resolve visits whose edits never conflicted, and those visits miss a Friday. This decision accepts a review queue larger than the conflict rate justifies as the price of a server that cannot silently combine observations. The maintenance criterion compounds the choice rather than opposing it — base-pointer comparison needs no upkeep, which suits a team with two coordinators and no systems administrator.
+
+What this forecloses: automatic combination of unrelated edits, and therefore any expectation that a visit edited twice reaches the export without a coordinator. Composition in the resolution step is what keeps that from costing data — a coordinator can produce the combined record by hand — but it is a human step every time.
+
+What it obligates downstream:
+
+- **Tablet-sync** retains, for every local draft, the ID of the revision it was edited from, across app restarts and multi-day disconnection, and sends it with the submission. It presents a contested visit as competing versions awaiting coordinator resolution rather than as an editable current record, and handles a rejected base revision ID — a stale or corrupt local record — distinctly from a transient submission failure, since the first is not usefully retried.
+- **Review-export** presents competing heads side by side with each one's author and receipt time, offers both resolution forms, and holds contested visits out of the Friday CSV. The export set is the settled visits; the number of visits held back is itself something coordinators need to see before Friday, since review backlog is export delay.
+- Both link to this node's contract rather than restating concurrency semantics.
+
+This decision is worth re-running if the review queue turns out to be dominated by visits with provably disjoint edits and the backlog delays the partner's data rather than merely occupying coordinators. The rule-maintenance and integrity costs of merging do not fall in that case, so the question would be narrower than the one settled here: whether a specific, small class of field changes — ones with no scientific relationship to any other field — can combine without review while everything else stays whole-visit.
